Azure Resource Groups provide a logical container for related Azure resources and can simplify lifecycle management. Resources placed in the same resource group can be managed collectively when appropriate.

Resource groups are commonly organized around:

* Applications
* Projects
* Environments
* Business functions
* Deployment lifecycles

For example, a development application might have its virtual machine, storage account, network interface, and supporting resources inside a dedicated resource group.

A real-life use case is a temporary testing environment that can be removed by deleting the resource group after testing is completed.

Resource groups simplify organization and lifecycle management, although resources do not necessarily have to share the same lifecycle merely because they belong to the same group.
